1
00:00:00,052 --> 00:00:01,602
October 2009.

2
00:00:01,978 --> 00:00:03,749
Chicago's O'Hare Airport.

3
00:00:04,167 --> 00:00:06,836
A man with one green eye and one brown is

4
00:00:06,849 --> 00:00:09,023
about to board a flight to Pakistan.

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,263
In his luggage are about thirteen

6
00:00:11,276 --> 00:00:13,867
surveillance videos, meant for the men who

7
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:14,518
sent him.

8
00:00:15,782 --> 00:00:16,785
He never boards.

9
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,270
FBI agents stop him first.

10
00:00:20,455 --> 00:00:23,176
Eleven months earlier, his scouting had

11
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:25,195
guided ten gunmen into Mumbai.

12
00:00:25,572 --> 00:00:28,203
He had scouted a second attack in Denmark.

13
00:00:28,619 --> 00:00:30,950
And for years before that, he had been

14
00:00:30,963 --> 00:00:32,916
working for the United States government.

15
00:00:34,152 --> 00:00:36,613
A paid informant for its drug agency.

16
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:40,022
In the same decade he trained with

17
00:00:40,022 --> 00:00:41,402
Lashkar-e-Taiba.

18
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:44,514
By his own testimony, he took money from

19
00:00:44,527 --> 00:00:47,248
an officer of Pakistan's intelligence

20
00:00:47,261 --> 00:00:47,821
service.

21
00:00:48,238 --> 00:00:50,477
And the United States was warned about him

22
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:51,662
again and again.

23
00:00:52,907 --> 00:00:54,235
At least five times.

24
00:00:55,479 --> 00:00:57,914
So how does a man flagged that often walk

25
00:00:57,927 --> 00:00:58,930
through every door?

26
00:00:59,347 --> 00:01:01,183
Everything here comes from the record —

27
00:01:01,209 --> 00:01:04,438
his guilty plea, US court filings, and his

28
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:06,379
own testimony to an Indian court.

29
00:01:07,647 --> 00:01:09,783
His name was David Coleman Headley.

30
00:01:10,199 --> 00:01:12,100
And he lived in the gaps between the

31
00:01:12,113 --> 00:01:13,845
agencies meant to catch him.

32
00:01:21,224 --> 00:01:24,232
He was born Daood Gilani, in Washington,

33
00:01:24,518 --> 00:01:26,758
D.C., in 1960.

34
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:29,805
His father was a Pakistani broadcaster.

35
00:01:30,222 --> 00:01:32,656
His mother was an American from a wealthy

36
00:01:32,669 --> 00:01:33,919
Philadelphia family.

37
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:36,865
Then came Pakistan.

38
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:38,664
He was still an infant.

39
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:41,906
As a boy he went to Cadet College

40
00:01:41,906 --> 00:01:42,987
Hasan Abdal,

41
00:01:43,534 --> 00:01:45,709
a military prep school in Punjab.

42
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:47,857
There he met a classmate named

43
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:50,604
Tahawwur Rana — the front man of our last

44
00:01:50,617 --> 00:01:51,047
file.

45
00:01:51,464 --> 00:01:54,159
At seventeen, Gilani moved back to America

46
00:01:54,172 --> 00:01:55,513
to live with his mother.

47
00:01:55,904 --> 00:01:57,727
He spoke Urdu and English.

48
00:01:58,130 --> 00:01:59,758
He could be charming in both.

49
00:02:00,923 --> 00:02:01,796
Two countries.

50
00:02:02,212 --> 00:02:03,254
Two languages.

51
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:05,977
Eventually, two names.

52
00:02:06,395 --> 00:02:08,270
He would later take his mother's maiden

53
00:02:08,283 --> 00:02:10,627
name, and become David Headley.

54
00:02:11,044 --> 00:02:13,895
One US counter-terrorism official, quoted

55
00:02:13,908 --> 00:02:15,783
by ProPublica and NBC,

56
00:02:16,382 --> 00:02:17,906
summed him up in a single line:

57
00:02:18,622 --> 00:02:21,057
"a mercenary, not ideologically driven."

58
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:23,753
Hold on to that word.

59
00:02:24,129 --> 00:02:26,147
It explains almost everything that

60
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:26,811
follows.

61
00:02:28,211 --> 00:02:30,151
The first thing Headley learned was that

62
00:02:30,164 --> 00:02:31,818
getting caught was negotiable.

63
00:02:33,004 --> 00:02:34,124
In 1988,

64
00:02:34,671 --> 00:02:37,132
German police in Frankfurt found two kilos

65
00:02:37,145 --> 00:02:38,629
of heroin in his luggage,

66
00:02:39,137 --> 00:02:40,543
carried from Pakistan.

67
00:02:40,999 --> 00:02:43,616
He cooperated with American drug agents

68
00:02:43,629 --> 00:02:45,009
and gave up his partners.

69
00:02:45,413 --> 00:02:47,041
He served about four years.

70
00:02:48,324 --> 00:02:49,939
His co-defendant got ten.

71
00:02:51,112 --> 00:02:52,974
Nine years later, according to court

72
00:02:52,987 --> 00:02:54,445
records, it happened again.

73
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:56,959
The Drug Enforcement Administration caught

74
00:02:56,972 --> 00:02:59,198
him in a heroin sting at a Manhattan

75
00:02:59,211 --> 00:02:59,797
hotel.

76
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:01,346
Again he traded.

77
00:03:01,763 --> 00:03:03,638
This time he became a confidential

78
00:03:03,651 --> 00:03:06,151
informant, and the agency sent him to

79
00:03:06,151 --> 00:03:07,974
Pakistan on its behalf.

80
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:10,748
He served about fifteen months instead of

81
00:03:10,761 --> 00:03:12,167
a possible nine years.

82
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,996
After the September 11 attacks, his

83
00:03:16,009 --> 00:03:17,989
handlers reportedly had him gather

84
00:03:18,002 --> 00:03:20,319
intelligence on extremists, not only drug

85
00:03:20,332 --> 00:03:20,905
networks.

86
00:03:21,322 --> 00:03:24,239
The DEA says his last contract ended in

87
00:03:24,252 --> 00:03:25,619
March 2002.

88
00:03:26,908 --> 00:03:29,057
Every time the system caught him, it found

89
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:30,502
a use for him instead.

90
00:03:31,647 --> 00:03:34,121
That same year, he walked into a

91
00:03:34,134 --> 00:03:36,751
Lashkar-e-Taiba gathering in Pakistan.

92
00:03:38,157 --> 00:03:39,837
He had come to hear a lecture by

93
00:03:39,837 --> 00:03:40,866
Hafiz Saeed,

94
00:03:41,504 --> 00:03:42,701
Lashkar's founder.

95
00:03:43,079 --> 00:03:43,704
He stayed.

96
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:46,308
According to the US Department of Justice,

97
00:03:46,634 --> 00:03:48,925
Headley went on to attend five Lashkar

98
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:51,868
training courses between 2002 and 2005.

99
00:03:53,054 --> 00:03:54,851
The first was a basic course in

100
00:03:54,851 --> 00:03:57,507
Muzaffarabad, in Illegally Occupied

101
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:58,796
Pakistani Kashmir.

102
00:03:59,172 --> 00:04:01,542
Accounts describe it as overseen by

103
00:04:01,555 --> 00:04:03,312
Lashkar's operations chief,

104
00:04:03,677 --> 00:04:05,305
Zaki-ur-Rehman Lakhvi.

105
00:04:05,723 --> 00:04:08,015
Later training took him to the Mansehra

106
00:04:08,015 --> 00:04:08,549
area.

107
00:04:08,924 --> 00:04:10,969
It is the same camp pipeline from the

108
00:04:10,982 --> 00:04:12,557
first file in this series.

109
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:15,721
Except this recruit held an American

110
00:04:15,734 --> 00:04:19,133
passport — and had, until recently, been

111
00:04:19,146 --> 00:04:21,242
on the payroll of an American agency.

112
00:04:22,416 --> 00:04:24,148
That passport was the point.

113
00:04:24,565 --> 00:04:27,729
In late 2005, three Lashkar members told

114
00:04:27,742 --> 00:04:29,512
him to go to India and conduct

115
00:04:29,526 --> 00:04:30,268
surveillance.

116
00:04:30,671 --> 00:04:33,080
The next year, he legally made the Western

117
00:04:33,093 --> 00:04:33,927
name his own.

118
00:04:35,164 --> 00:04:37,807
A David Headley draws fewer questions at

119
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:40,229
an Indian border than a Daood Gilani.

120
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:43,417
His handler was Sajid Mir.

121
00:04:43,836 --> 00:04:46,427
Three years later, Mir would be one of the

122
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:48,667
voices in the Karachi control room,

123
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:50,685
directing the gunmen by phone.

124
00:04:52,091 --> 00:04:54,604
Every spy needs a reason to be somewhere.

125
00:04:55,021 --> 00:04:57,234
Headley's came from his oldest friend.

126
00:04:57,651 --> 00:04:59,318
He opened a Mumbai branch of

127
00:04:59,318 --> 00:05:01,896
Tahawwur Rana's immigration consultancy,

128
00:05:02,404 --> 00:05:04,214
and used it as his cover story.

129
00:05:05,378 --> 00:05:08,438
Between 2006 and 2008 he made five

130
00:05:08,451 --> 00:05:10,079
surveillance trips to the city.

131
00:05:10,482 --> 00:05:11,146
He filmed.

132
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:12,683
He walked the routes.

133
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,183
He marked targets with GPS.

134
00:05:16,437 --> 00:05:18,325
He did not hide in cheap rooms.

135
00:05:18,755 --> 00:05:21,138
He checked into the Taj Mahal Palace and

136
00:05:21,151 --> 00:05:23,351
the Oberoi-Trident — the hotels that would

137
00:05:23,364 --> 00:05:25,291
become the main stage of the siege.

138
00:05:26,462 --> 00:05:29,170
He found a gym, and friends — among them

139
00:05:29,184 --> 00:05:30,811
the son of a well-known Bollywood

140
00:05:30,824 --> 00:05:32,920
filmmaker, who showed him around the city.

141
00:05:34,187 --> 00:05:36,453
To anyone watching, he was an American

142
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:38,328
businessman enjoying Mumbai.

143
00:05:39,495 --> 00:05:41,631
In court years later, Headley testified

144
00:05:41,644 --> 00:05:43,427
that Lashkar had weighed other targets

145
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:43,831
too —

146
00:05:44,391 --> 00:05:46,292
a famous temple, and a conference of

147
00:05:46,305 --> 00:05:48,037
defence scientists at the Taj.

148
00:05:48,466 --> 00:05:50,901
He also said two earlier attempts by sea

149
00:05:50,914 --> 00:05:54,026
had failed, in September and October 2008,

150
00:05:54,052 --> 00:05:55,133
because of accidents.

151
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:57,608
Two attempts failed.

152
00:05:57,986 --> 00:05:59,105
The third did not.

153
00:06:00,317 --> 00:06:02,713
When ten men came ashore in Mumbai that

154
00:06:02,726 --> 00:06:04,874
November, they were working from his

155
00:06:04,888 --> 00:06:05,734
reconnaissance.

156
00:06:07,124 --> 00:06:09,507
This is where the story stops being only a

157
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:10,093
crime.

158
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,363
A review led by the US Director of

159
00:06:13,377 --> 00:06:14,509
National Intelligence,

160
00:06:14,978 --> 00:06:17,205
reported by ProPublica and The Washington

161
00:06:17,218 --> 00:06:19,587
Post, found at least five separate

162
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:20,147
warnings.

163
00:06:20,564 --> 00:06:22,556
People close to Headley told American

164
00:06:22,569 --> 00:06:24,431
authorities he was training with, or

165
00:06:24,444 --> 00:06:26,475
working for, Pakistani militants.

166
00:06:27,752 --> 00:06:29,640
Some of them were his own wives.

167
00:06:30,828 --> 00:06:33,940
In 2005, after a domestic dispute, his

168
00:06:33,953 --> 00:06:36,609
American wife went to FBI agents in New

169
00:06:36,622 --> 00:06:37,143
York.

170
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:39,565
She told them he was an active Lashkar

171
00:06:39,578 --> 00:06:41,805
militant who had trained in its camps.

172
00:06:42,195 --> 00:06:44,643
The FBI says it looked into the tip.

173
00:06:45,906 --> 00:06:47,065
Nothing came of it.

174
00:06:48,504 --> 00:06:50,822
In 2007, his Moroccan wife,

175
00:06:51,213 --> 00:06:54,676
Faiza Outalha, went to the US Embassy

176
00:06:54,689 --> 00:06:55,848
in Islamabad.

177
00:06:56,225 --> 00:06:56,668
Twice.

178
00:06:57,098 --> 00:06:59,390
She brought photographs of their stays at

179
00:06:59,403 --> 00:07:00,939
the Taj and the Oberoi.

180
00:07:01,356 --> 00:07:03,712
Her words to the officials, as reported:

181
00:07:04,377 --> 00:07:06,603
"He's either a terrorist, or he's working

182
00:07:06,616 --> 00:07:07,046
for you."

183
00:07:08,392 --> 00:07:10,254
She was not taken seriously.

184
00:07:11,423 --> 00:07:14,548
There were more tips — in 2001, in 2002,

185
00:07:14,822 --> 00:07:16,462
and in April 2008.

186
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:19,952
The last one arrived in December 2008.

187
00:07:21,197 --> 00:07:22,825
A month after the attack.

188
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,357
US officials say the tips were too general

189
00:07:26,370 --> 00:07:28,948
to link him to a specific plot, and that

190
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:30,524
with specifics they would have warned

191
00:07:30,537 --> 00:07:31,005
India.

192
00:07:31,435 --> 00:07:34,404
Headley was stopped only in October 2009,

193
00:07:34,912 --> 00:07:36,852
after British intelligence flagged his

194
00:07:36,865 --> 00:07:39,182
contacts with al-Qaeda figures in Europe.

195
00:07:40,459 --> 00:07:42,972
The documented failure is missed warnings.

196
00:07:43,376 --> 00:07:45,902
That anyone protected him on purpose has

197
00:07:45,915 --> 00:07:46,943
never been shown.

198
00:07:48,331 --> 00:07:50,336
Mumbai did not end his work.

199
00:07:50,712 --> 00:07:52,014
It raised his price.

200
00:07:53,185 --> 00:07:56,440
In 2009 he was back in India, scouting new

201
00:07:56,453 --> 00:07:58,732
targets, including Delhi's National

202
00:07:58,745 --> 00:07:59,709
Defence College.

203
00:08:00,099 --> 00:08:01,701
Then he turned to Denmark.

204
00:08:02,156 --> 00:08:04,643
His target was Jyllands-Posten, the

205
00:08:04,656 --> 00:08:07,013
newspaper that had published cartoons of

206
00:08:07,026 --> 00:08:08,159
the Prophet Muhammad.

207
00:08:08,615 --> 00:08:10,867
He scouted its offices the way he had

208
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:12,039
